class Solution {
  public int[] paidReservationsPositiveProofStrip(int[][] drawers, int k) {
    int[][] a=drawers; List<Integer> out=new ArrayList<>();
    {int[][]rows=a.clone();Arrays.sort(rows,Comparator.comparingInt((int[]r)->r[1]).thenComparingInt(r->r[0]).thenComparingInt(r->r[2]));int[]dp=new int[rows.length+1];for(int i=0;i<rows.length;i++){int l=0,r=i;while(l<r){int m=(l+r)/2;if(rows[m][1]<=rows[i][0])l=m+1;else r=m;}dp[i+1]=Math.max(dp[i],dp[l]+rows[i][2]);out.add(dp[i+1]);}}
    return out.stream().filter(x->x>0).mapToInt(Integer::intValue).toArray();
  }
}

privateData.editorialMd:
The working idea

Each reservation is [start,end,reward], with start < end and nonnegative reward, occupying [start,end). Sort by end, then start, then reward. For each nonempty prefix of this sorted list, produce the maximum reward from nonoverlapping reservations in that prefix. Touching endpoints are compatible.

Return only the strictly positive values in the derived sequence, preserving their relative order and duplicates.

Why this works

Each derived value follows the unit's stated order and includes exactly the items allowed by its definition. The receipt then folds or filters those values without changing their meaning. Check the empty, singleton, repeated and maximum-size inputs before trusting the final total.

Target time O(n log n); space O(n).

